{'review': '<<<ARTIFACT review_report>>>\n{\n  "overall_status": "NEEDS_FIXES",\n  "quality_score": 3.5,\n  "summary": {\n    "critical": 0,\n    "high": 1,\n    "medium": 4,\n    "low": 1,\n    "passed_checks": [\n      "Type annotations",\n      "No hardcoded secrets",\n      "Loading state management",\n      "Logout functionality"\n    ]\n  },\n  "findings": [\n    {\n      "finding_id": "F-001",\n      "severity": "high",\n      "category": "performance",\n      "title": "Functions recreated on every render causing unnecessary re-renders",\n      "file": "src/context/AuthContext.tsx",\n      "line_range": [28, 62],\n      "description": "The login, register, and logout functions are defined inside the AuthProvider component and are recreated on every render. This can cause unnecessary re-renders in consumer components that use these functions as dependencies in useEffect or useMemo.",\n      "impact": "Consumer components may re-render more frequently than necessary, leading to performance degradation.",\n      "suggested_fix": "Wrap the login, register, and logout functions with useCallback to memoize them. Specify the dependencies appropriately (e.g., for login and register, the dependencies are empty because they don\'t rely on any props or state from the component? Actually, they do set state, but the setState functions are stable. However, note that the setState functions from useState are stable and don\'t change, so we can use an empty dependency array).",\n      "code_before": "  const login = async (email: string, password: string) => {\\n    setLoading(true);\\n    setError(null);\\n    try {\\n      const response = await apiLogin({ email, password });\\n      handleAuthSuccess(response.data, email);\\n    } catch (err: any) {\\n      setError(err.response?.data?.detail || \'Login failed\');\\n      throw err;\\n    } finally {\\n      setLoading(false);\\n    }\\n  }\\n\\n  const register = async (email: string, password: string) => {\\n    setLoading(true);\\n    setError(null);\\n    try {\\n      const response = await apiRegister({ email, password });\\n      handleAuthSuccess(response.data, email);\\n    } catch (err: any) {\\n      setError(err.response?.data?.detail || \'Registration failed\');\\n      throw err;\\n    } finally {\\n      setLoading(false);\\n    }\\n  }\\n\\n  const logout = () => {\\n    localStorage.removeItem(\'jwt_token\');\\n    setToken(null);\\n    setIsAuthenticated(false);\\n    setUser(null);\\n    setError(null);\\n  };",\n      "code_after": "  const login = useCallback(async (email: string, password: string) => {\\n    setLoading(true);\\n    setError(null);\\n    try {\\n      const response = await apiLogin({ email, password });\\n      handleAuthSuccess(response.data, email);\\n    } catch (err: any) {\\n      setError(err.response?.data?.detail || \'Login failed\');\\n      throw err;\\n    } finally {\\n      setLoading(false);\\n    }\\n  }, []);\\n\\n  const register = useCallback(async (email: string, password: string) => {\\n    setLoading(true);\\n    setError(null);\\n    try {\\n      const response = await apiRegister({ email, password });\\n      handleAuthSuccess(response.data, email);\\n    } catch (err: any) {\\n      setError(err.response?.data?.detail || \'Registration failed\');\\n      throw err;\\n    } finally {\\n      setLoading(false);\\n    }\\n  }, []);\\n\\n  const logout = useCallback(() => {\\n    localStorage.removeItem(\'jwt_token\');\\n    setToken(null);\\n    setIsAuthenticated(false);\\n    setUser(null);\\n    setError(null);\\n  }, []);",\n      "references": [\n        "https://reactjs.org/docs/hooks-reference.html#usecallback"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      "finding_id": "F-002",\n      "severity": "medium",\n      "category": "quality",\n      "title": "Duplicated code in login and register functions",\n      "file": "src/context/AuthContext.tsx",\n      "line_range": [28, 54],\n      "description": "The login and register functions have nearly identical structure, differing only in the API call (apiLogin vs apiRegister) and the error message. This duplication violates the DRY principle and makes maintenance harder.",\n      "impact": "Changes to the authentication flow (e.g., changing how the token is stored or how the user is set) must be made in two places, increasing the risk of inconsistencies.",\n      "suggested_fix": "Extract the common logic into a helper function that takes the API call function and the success/error messages as parameters.",\n      "code_before": "  const login = async (email: string, password: string) => {\\n    setLoading(true);\\n    setError(null);\\n    try {\\n      const response = await apiLogin({ email, password });\\n      handleAuthSuccess(response.data, email);\\n    } catch (err: any) {\\n      setError(err.response?.data?.detail || \'Login failed\');\\n      throw err;\\n    } finally {\\n      setLoading(false);\\n    }\\n  }\\n\\n  const register = async (email: string, password: string) => {\\n    setLoading(true);\\n    setError(null);\\n    try {\\n      const response = await apiRegister({ email, password });\\n      handleAuthSuccess(response.data, email);\\n    } catch (err: any) {\\n      setError(err.response?.data?.detail || \'Registration failed\');\\n      throw err;\\n    } finally {\\n      setLoading(false);\\n    }\\n  };",\n      "code_after": "  const authenticate = async (\\n    apiCall: (credentials: { email: string; password: string }) => Promise<{ data: AuthResponse }>,\\n    successMessage: string,\\n    errorMessage: string\\n  ) => {\\n    setLoading(true);\\n    setError(null);\\n    try {\\n      const response = await apiCall({ email, password });\\n      handleAuthSuccess(response.data, email);\\n    } catch (err: any) {\\n      setError(err.response?.data?.detail || errorMessage);\\n      throw err;\\n    } finally {\\n      setLoading(false);\\n    }\\n  };\\n\\n  const login = (email: string, password: string) => authenticate(apiLogin, \'Login successful\', \'Login failed\');\\n  const register = (email: string, password: string) => authenticate(apiRegister, \'Registration successful\', \'Registration failed\');",\n      "references": []\n    },\n    {\n      "finding_id": "F-003",\n      "severity": "medium",\n      "category": "quality",\n      "title": "Error handling assumes specific API error structure",\n      "file": "src/context/AuthContext.tsx",\n      "line_range": [34, 36],\n      "description": "The error handling in the login and register functions assumes that the error object has a response property with a data property that contains a detail string. This is specific to certain backend frameworks (like Django) and may break if the API returns a different error structure.",\n      "impact": "If the API error format changes, the application may not display an appropriate error message to the user, showing a generic \'Login failed\' or \'Registration failed\' even when more specific information is available.",\n      "suggested_fix": "Create a helper function to extract the error message from the error object in a more robust way, or at least check for the existence of the properties before accessing them.",\n      "code_before": "    } catch (err: any) {\\n      setError(err.response?.data?.detail || \'Login failed\');\\n      throw err;\\n    }",\n      "code_after": "    } catch (err: any) {\\n      const message = err.response?.data?.detail || err.message || \'Login failed\';\\n      setError(message);\\n      throw err;\\n    }",\n      "references": []\n    },\n    {\n      "finding_id": "F-004",\n      "severity": "low",\n      "category": "quality",\n      "title": "Hardcoded dummy user placeholder",\n      "file": "src/context/AuthContext.tsx",\n      "line_range": [18, 20],\n      "description": "In the useEffect that runs on mount, when a token is found in localStorage, the user is set to a hardcoded dummy user instead of fetching the actual user data or decoding the token to get user information.",\n      "impact": "The application displays incorrect user information (always the dummy user) for authenticated users, which is a placeholder and not suitable for production.",\n      "suggested_fix": "Replace the dummy user with a call to an API endpoint to fetch the user profile using the token, or decode the token (if it\'s a JWT) to extract user information (note: decoding the token on the client is safe but does not guarantee the token is valid; the backend should still validate it).",\n      "code_before": "    if (storedToken) {\\n      setToken(storedToken);\\n      setIsAuthenticated(true);\\n      // In a real app, you\'d decode the token or make an API call to get user info\\n      // For simplicity, we\'ll just set a dummy user for now if token exists\\n      setUser({ id: \'dummy-id\', email: \'user@example.com\' });\\n    }",\n      "code_after": "    if (storedToken) {\\n      setToken(storedToken);\\n      setIsAuthenticated(true);\\n      // Fetch user info from the API using the token\\n      try {\\n        const userResponse = await apiGetUserProfile();\\n        setUser(userResponse.data);\\n      } catch (err) {\\n        // If fetching user fails, clear the token and log out\\n        console.error(\'Failed to fetch user profile\', err);\\n        logout();\\n      }\\n    }",\n      "references": []\n    },\n    {\n      "finding_id": "F-005",\n      "severity": "medium",\n      "category": "quality",\n      "title": "No token expiration check when reading from localStorage",\n      "file": "src/context/AuthContext.tsx",\n      "line_range": [13, 17],\n      "description": "When the application loads, it checks for a token in localStorage and immediately sets the user as authenticated without verifying if the token is still valid (e.g., not expired). This could lead to the application considering a user authenticated when their token has actually expired.",\n      "impact": "The application may allow access to protected routes or show authenticated UI for an expired token, leading to errors when making API calls (which will likely return 401) and a poor user experience.",\n      "suggested_fix": "Decode the token (if it\'s a JWT) to check its expiration time, or make an API call to validate the token (e.g., a /me endpoint) and only set the authenticated state if the token is valid.",\n      "code_before": "  useEffect(() => {\\n    const storedToken = localStorage.getItem(\'jwt_token\');\\n    if (storedToken) {\\n      setToken(storedToken);\\n      setIsAuthenticated(true);\\n      // In a real app, you\'d decode the token or make an API call to get user info\\n      // For simplicity, we\'ll just set a dummy user for now if token exists\\n      setUser({ id: \'dummy-id\', email: \'user@example.com\' });\\n    }\\n    setLoading(false);\\n  }, []);",\n      "code_after": "  useEffect(() => {\\n    const storedToken = localStorage.getItem(\'jwt_token\');\\n    if (storedToken) {\\n      // Option 1: Decode the token (if JWT) to check expiration\\n      try {\\n        const payload = JSON.parse(atob(storedToken.split(\'.\')[1]));\\n        const now = Math.floor(Date.now() / 1000);\\n        if (payload.exp > now) {\\n          setToken(storedToken);\\n          setIsAuthenticated(true);\\n          // Fetch user info or set user from token payload\\n          setUser({ id: payload.sub, email: payload.email }); // example\\n        } else {\\n          // Token expired\\n          logout();\\n        }\\n      } catch (e) {\\n        // Invalid token\\n        logout();\\n      }\\n      // Option 2: Make an API call to validate the token (uncomment if preferred)\\n      // validateToken(storedToken).then(isValid => {\\n      //   if (isValid) {\\n      //     setToken(storedToken);\\n      //     setIsAuthenticated(true);\\n      //     // fetch user\\n      //   } else {\\n      //     logout();\\n      //   }\\n      // }).finally(() => setLoading(false));\\n    } else {\\n      setLoading(false);\\n    }\\n  }, []);",\n      "references": [\n        "https://jwt.io/introduction/"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      "finding_id": "F-006",\n      "severity": "medium",\n      "category": "security",\n      "title": "Token stored in localStorage vulnerable to XSS",\n      "file": "src/context/AuthContext.tsx",\n      "line_range": [13, 17],\n      "description": "The authentication token is stored in localStorage, which is accessible via JavaScript. This makes the application vulnerable to Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) attacks. If an attacker can execute arbitrary JavaScript in the context of the application (e.g., through a malicious third-party script or a vulnerability in the app), they can steal the token and impersonate the user.",\n      "impact": "Token theft leading to account compromise. An attacker who steals the token can make authenticated requests as the user until the token expires or is revoked.",\n      "suggested_fix": "Consider using HttpOnly cookies to store the token, which are not accessible via JavaScript. This requires changes to the backend to set the cookie and the frontend to send the cookie with requests (which the browser does automatically). If localStorage must be used, implement strict Content Security Policy (CSP) to mitigate XSS risks.",\n      "code_before": "    const storedToken = localStorage.getItem(\'jwt_token\');\\n    ...\\n    localStorage.setItem(\'jwt_token\', authResponse.access_token);\\n    ...\\n    localStorage.removeItem(\'jwt_token\');",\n      "code_after": "// Note: This is a conceptual change. The actual implementation would require backend support.\\n// Instead of localStorage, use HttpOnly cookies.\\n// Example of setting a cookie (requires backend to set Set-Cookie header):\\n// document.cookie = `token=${authResponse.access_token}; HttpOnly; Secure; SameSite=Strict`;\\n// However, note: managing cookies for authentication in SPAs is more complex and often requires backend cooperation.\\n// For the purpose of this review, we note the risk and recommend switching to cookies if possible.\\n// If localStorage must be retained, ensure strong XSS protections are in place (CSP, sanitization, etc.).",\n      "references": [\n        "https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Authentication_Cheat_Sheet.html#stateless-authentication"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  "positive_observations": [\n    "Uses TypeScript with proper type annotations for the context and provider.",\n    "No hardcoded secrets (like API keys) in the code.",\n    "Proper error handling with try/catch and setting error state.",\n    "Loading state is managed correctly in login and register.",\n    "The logout function properly clears the token and state."\n  ]\n}\n<<<ENDARTIFACT>>>\n```'}